<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Technology in Education – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology in Education</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> When It's Better to Ignore Technology</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe a recent personal experience. Interactive: Exchange two or more ideas in group discussions on familiar subjects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe and recommend an educational app during a group discussion based on a recent personal experience (e.g., "I used a language learning app for language practice, and it helped me learn new vocabulary easily.").
Can exchange ideas in group discussions about useful technology in school by sharing personal preferences and comparing features (e.g., "I prefer tablets for reading because they are light and easy to use, but laptops are better for writing reports.").
Can participate in discussions about technology limitations by suggesting solutions or alternatives (e.g., "We should limit technology use during lunch to talk more with friends. Instead, we can play outdoor games to stay active.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to stand up if they agree with simple statements about technology. For example: "Stand up if you use your phone for learning," "Stand up if you think we should ignore our phones during family meals," "Stand up if you prefer reading digital books instead of physical ones." This gets students moving and expressing initial preferences. The teacher encourages quick explanations from 2-3 students for their choices (e.g., "I prefer digital books because they are more accessible.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing opinions and making suggestions using target grammar. "I think technology is beneficial for learning, but sometimes we should limit its use. For example, I can browse information quickly, but I should ignore notifications when I'm studying. I prefer traditional notebooks for writing because I find them more effective." The teacher highlights stress in "beneficial" and "effective."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and grammar:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'ignore' mean? Does it mean 'pay attention to' or 'not pay attention to'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'You should limit screen time,' am I giving an order or a suggestion?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's the difference between 'can' and 'should'? Can I use my phone for calls? Should I use it during class?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (1.5-2 minutes) of two teenagers discussing "When and where should we ignore technology in school?". The dialogue includes opinions, comparisons, suggestions, and vocabulary like <i>apps, beneficial, problem, solution, prefer, limit, recommend, device, network</i>. The recording also features examples of stress in "education" and "information," and intonation patterns for "should" and "can."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will listen to identify different perspectives on appropriate technology use and suggestions for limiting it. This builds their foundation for engaging in similar conversations and weighing benefits and drawbacks, as well as suggesting limits.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main topic of the conversation?" "Do the speakers agree or disagree about using technology during lunch?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen again and mark statements as true or false, e.g., "One speaker thinks educational apps are always useful." "They recommend ignoring devices during presentations."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match each speaker's suggestion (e.g., "limit phone use in the cafeteria") with their reason (e.g., "to talk more with friends").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using simple present for expressing opinions and comparing preferences, modals "should" for suggestions and "can" for ability, and Wh-questions for discussions. Target vocabulary includes: <i>technology, apps, tools, benefit, problem, solution, device, prefer, ignore, limit, recommend</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion:</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences. They complete them using 'should', 'can', or simple present verbs (e.g., 'prefer', 'use', 'ignore') and relevant vocabulary.
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "We _____ (limit) our screen time while reading to avoid distractions." "I _____ (prefer) interactive apps, _____ (but/while) my friend _____ (like) traditional books." "What _____ (apps/tools) do you _____ (recommend) for improving English?" The teacher monitors pronunciation, especially stress in words like "distractions" and intonation with modals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Opinion Exchange Chain:</b> In small groups, students take turns expressing opinions and making suggestions. One student starts with a statement, e.g., "I think tablets are useful for research." The next student responds, "Yes, I agree. Tablets are accessible, but we should also use books because they are better for our eyes." The next student adds, "I prefer laptops for writing reports because they are faster." Students are prompted to use "I prefer... but...", "We should...", "I can...", and "What...?" questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Tech Talk Cards:</b> Students work in pairs. They receive 'Tech Talk' cards with different scenarios or questions about technology in education. Examples: "Describe an app you use for school and why you recommend it." "When should students ignore their phones during class? Give a solution." "Compare benefits and problems of using computers vs. notebooks for note-taking." Students ask and answer, exchanging at least two ideas per question, using the target vocabulary and grammar. The teacher circulates, providing feedback on accuracy and prompting for more elaborate responses, focusing on minimal pairs like /ʊ/ in "should" and /uː/ in "shooed" if students mispronounce.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Discussion – "Our School's Tech Policy"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of 3-4. Each group discusses creating a "Tech Policy" for their school, focusing on when technology should be used and when it should be ignored. The discussion prompts them to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describe a recent personal experience where technology was (or wasn't) beneficial for their learning, and how they used it. (e.g., "I used a language learning app for language practice, and it helped me learn new vocabulary easily.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recommend an educational app or digital tool and explain why it's useful.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Exchange ideas about useful technology in school, sharing preferences and comparing features (e.g., "I prefer tablets for reading because they are light and easy to use, but laptops are better for writing reports.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Propose solutions or alternatives for managing technology limitations (e.g., "We should limit technology use during lunch to talk more with friends. Instead, we can play outdoor games to stay active.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students must use target grammar (simple present, 'should', 'can', Wh-questions), vocabulary (<i>benefit, problem, solution, prefer, ignore, limit, recommend</i>), and pragmatic functions (weighing benefits/drawbacks, suggesting limits, expressing opinions). The teacher observes, facilitating only when necessary to encourage all students to participate and maintain fluency.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</b> The teacher uses a checklist to note student participation, use of target language, and fluency. Students also use a simplified peer assessment checklist to evaluate their group members' contributions to the discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate actively in the "Our School's Tech Policy" group discussion, demonstrating their ability to describe experiences, recommend apps, compare preferences, and suggest solutions/alternatives regarding technology use in school, adhering to the learning outcomes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Intelligibility:</b> Ideas are clear and easy to understand. Pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., "innovative," "information") and intonation with modals ("should," "can") is intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately uses nouns (<i>apps, benefits, problems, solutions, devices</i>), verbs (<i>prefer, ignore, limit, recommend</i>), and adjectives (<i>useful, beneficial</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correctly uses simple present for opinions, modals "should" and "can" for suggestions/ability, and forms Wh-questions for interaction.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and Interaction:</b> Actively exchanges two or more ideas, responds to peers, and contributes to the group's discussion goals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Successfully describes experiences, recommends apps, compares preferences, and suggests solutions related to technology in education.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about using technology appropriately is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One suggestion I gave in the discussion was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One challenge I had while speaking in English today was..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I do well in today's discussion about technology?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific grammar (e.g., 'should', simple present) or vocabulary can I improve to better express my opinions or make suggestions next time?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What speaking activities worked particularly well for encouraging natural interaction and use of target language? Was the group discussion engaging?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students struggled with expressing complex ideas or using modals accurately? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., sentence starters, more controlled practice) are needed for future speaking tasks?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully connect their personal experiences to the broader theme of appropriate technology use? Was the pronunciation focus effective?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking practice of exchanging ideas, describing experiences, and making suggestions about technology provides a strong foundation for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition). Students will use these same ideas, vocabulary, and grammatical structures to write a short opinion piece or a set of recommendations about technology use in school, building on the fluency gained today towards written accuracy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>